%[text] %[text:anchor:T_EF876BD9] # "MATLAB-SimBiology-DevOps-Workflow-Example" Workshop Guide
%[text] 
%[text] This workshop provides a step-by-step guide to generating automated tests using the MATLAB Unit Test Framework and MATLAB Test.
%[text] 
%[text] <u>In this workshop, you will:</u>
%[text] - fork and modify your own copy of the "[MATLAB-SimBiology-DevOps-Workflow-Example](https://github.com/simkaryote/MATLAB-SimBiology-DevOps-Workflow-Example)" repository on GitHub
%[text] - generate tests using your command history and MATLAB Copilot
%[text] - automatically find and run existing tests
%[text] - explore code coverage metrics for your tested code
%[text] - automate your testing using continuous integration (CI) practices with GitHub Actions and build tool \
%[text] 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_34C2FB57] ## Workshop Requirements
%[text] 
%[text] The following steps cover all of the things you will need to successfully complete the workshop:
%[text] 
%[text] **A)  Access to MATLAB Online or a desktop MATLAB with the MATLAB Test*****™*** **product**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D43A4973] - Go to:  [**https://matlab.mathworks.com**](https://matlab.mathworks.com/)
%[text] - <u>*Note:*</u>  If you do not have a MathWorks account, the above link will ask you to create an account for free \
%[text] **B)  Access to MATLAB Online or a desktop MATLAB with MATLAB Copilot**
%[text] - <u>*Note:*</u>  If you do not have a license for MATLAB Copilot, you can simply skip that part of the workshop \
%[text] **C)  A GitHub account**
%[text] - The workshop leverages the free repository and CI capabilities offered by GitHub and GitHub Actions
%[text] - Go to:  [**https://github.com/signup**](https://github.com/signup) \
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ## Part 1:  Getting the workshop files and configuring GitHub for automated testing and results publishing
%[text] 
%[text] <u>In this section, you will:</u>
%[text] 1. Fork the workshop files to your personal GitHub account
%[text] 2. Enable GitHub Actions for automated testing
%[text] 3. Enable GitHub Pages for report publishing
%[text] 4. Open the workshop in MATLAB  \
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_35a2] <u>**Part 1.1:  Fork the "Generating Tests for Your MATLAB Code Workshop" repository to your GitHub Account**</u>
%[text] 
%[text] First, you'll start by forking the "[MATLAB-SimBiology-DevOps-Workflow-Example](https://github.com/simkaryote/MATLAB-SimBiology-DevOps-Workflow-Example)" repository to our own GitHub account.
%[text] 
%[text] **Wait... What does it mean to "fork a repository" and why should I do it?**
%[text] Forking a repository allows you to freely experiment with and push changes to a project without affecting the original project.
%[text] GitHub provides some great information about "why" and "how" to fork a repository here: [https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/pull-requests/collaborating-with-pull-requests/working-with-forks/fork-a-repo](https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/pull-requests/collaborating-with-pull-requests/working-with-forks/fork-a-repo)
%[text] 
%[text] **However**, In our case, we will use forking as a way for everyone to have his/her own remote repository as everyone would be pushing the same changes to one single repo otherwise.
%[text] Since the forked repo is not meant to be pushed back to the original repository, we will make it standalone.
%[text] 
%[text] Let's get started!
%[text] 
%[text] **Go to:** [**https://github.com/simkaryote/MATLAB-SimBiology-DevOps-Workflow-Example**](https://github.com/simkaryote/MATLAB-SimBiology-DevOps-Workflow-Example)
%[text] ![](text:image:079f)
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4B8B9F5D] **Press the "Fork" button (top right)**
%[text] ![](text:image:9d4b)
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DB2C80D4] **Create your fork**
%[text] GitHub offers the ability to rename your copy of the repository, but this guide will assume the default name.
%[text] ![](text:image:1b71)
%[text] 
%[text] <u>***Notes:***</u>
%[text] - You may need to log into your GitHub account again during this step
%[text] - There's no problem with renaming your copy of the repository, but the rest of the workshop guide and screenshots assume the default repository name  \
%[text] 
%[text] 
%[text] **Once you've forked the repository, you should see:**
%[text] - your GitHub username at the top of your repository
%[text] - "forked from simkaryote/MATLAB-SimBiology-DevOps-Workflow-Example" \
%[text] ![](text:image:1f89)
%[text] 
%[text] **Let's make it now standalone for the sake of the workshop.** 
%[text] ![](text:image:6116)
%[text] ![](text:image:0cfa)
%[text] Now your repository is standalone and not linked to the original repository. 
%[text] Before you go to MATLAB, let's enable some useful GitHub features that will be automatically triggered when we push our changes to GitHub later.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_8dc4] <u>**Part 1.2:  Enable GitHub Actions**</u>
%[text] 
%[text] GitHub offers built-in continuous integration (CI) services via their "GitHub Actions" feature. By default, GitHub Actions is disabled for forked repositories that contain a GitHub Actions YAML file, so you'll have to enable Actions before you can use them for the workshop. The workshop already provides a GitHub Actions YAML file to automatically test your code and publish test results so you can see them directly from your repository every time you push your code.
%[text] 
%[text] **Select the "Actions" tab**
%[text] ![](text:image:832f)
%[text] 
%[text] **Click the "I understand my workflows, go ahead and enable them" button**
%[text] ![](text:image:4536)
%[text] 
%[text] And that's it! GitHub Actions is now enabled for your repository.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_2b25] <u>**Part 1.3:  Enable GitHub Pages to publish our test results**</u>
%[text] 
%[text] GitHub offers a built-in way to publish test reports and artifacts created from your CI jobs. By default, GitHub Pages is disabled for new repositories, so we'll have to enable GitHub Pages before we can publish our test reports.
%[text] 
%[text] **Select the "Settings" tab**
%[text] ![](text:image:2571)
%[text] 
%[text] **Select the "Pages" entry on the left-side navigation panel**
%[text] ![](text:image:813b)
%[text] 
%[text] **Change the "Build and deployment \> Source" dropdown and select "GitHub Actions"**
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:7a16)
%[text] GitHub Pages is now enabled and your GitHub Actions will be allowed to publish test results for everyone to see.
%[text] 
%[text] 
%%
